mode: "100644"
text: "# The reality of Suno in 2026 (verify-quarterly)\n\nThe legal landscape here moves monthly — attribute everything, **verify-quarterly** against suno.com's live\nTerms/Help Center, and treat pre-Warner-deal reviews as stale on rights.\n\n## What it is (and the moment it's in)\nSuno (v5.5, shipped 26 Mar 2026) generates full songs — vocals, lyrics, instrumentation — from text, with the\nmost natural-sounding vocals in the category (Udio wins on instrumental mix polish but became a no-download\n\"walled garden\" after its UMG settlement). The product now includes **Suno Studio** (Premier browser DAW —\nmultitrack, up to 12 stems rebuilt from the latent model with zero bleed, MIDI export, Warp Markers),\n**Personas** (reusable voice/style), **Voices** (clone your **own verified** singing voice), **Custom Models**\n(fine-tune v5.5 on 6+ tracks you verifiably own; up to 3, private), **My Taste** (preference learning), stems\n(Auto Split / Split from Mix), Extend, hum-to-song input, and an API (Python/Node; Zapier/Make) — API tier\ngating conflicts across sources; verify. (attribute: eesel, jamesm.blog, blakecrosley, margabagus)\n\n## The legal spine (the reason this skill exists — attribute; verify-quarterly)\n- **June 2024:** RIAA sued Suno + Udio for UMG/Sony/WMG. **Nov 2025: Warner settled + partnered** (first\n  major-label AI-music licensing deal; Suno acquired Songkick). **UMG and Sony are still suing Suno** — a\n  pivotal fair-use summary-judgment hearing is set for **July 2026** (D. Mass.); after fingerprinting evidence,\n  UMG/Sony moved (May 2026) to **add 61k+ recordings** to the case — Suno is opposing (attribute: MBW);\n  Germany's GEMA case verdict was scheduled June 2026.\n- **The Warner deal rewrote user rights:** ToS softened from \"you own it\" to users being **granted commercial\n  rights** (Suno's wording: users are \"generally not considered the owner\"); free-tier commercial downloads\n  removed (play/share); paid download caps described as forthcoming; **pre-deal models scheduled for\n  deprecation** when licensed models ship later in 2026 — **archive WAVs/stems/MIDI now**; artist **opt-in**\n  (voice/likeness, compensated, artist-controlled) announced.\n- **No retroactive rights:** free-tier songs stay non-commercial **forever** — upgrading later does not unlock\n  them. Generate on a paid plan from the start.\n- **No indemnification:** commercial rights ≠ legal defense — if a track draws an infringement claim, the risk\n  sits with the user (indemnification runs user → Suno).\n- **The protectability trap:** Suno's own docs state 100%-AI music **doesn't qualify for copyright** (prompting\n  isn't authorship; the Thaler v. Perlmutter human-authorship line). You can monetize, but stopping someone\n  re-using your raw track is legally weak — **human elements** (original lyrics, re-recorded parts over stems,\n  real arrangement in a DAW) strengthen the claim and reclassify the work as **AI-assisted** for distributors.\n- **DDEX AI-disclosure:** since late 2025, Spotify/Apple enforce AI flagging at distribution; don't register\n  purely-AI works with PROs (ASCAP/BMI).\n\n## Tiers + credits (≈; verify in-app)\n**Free:** 50 credits/day (~10 songs) — strictly non-commercial, forever. **Pro ≈ $8/mo annual (~$10 monthly):**\n2,500 credits (~500 songs), commercial rights, Personas, Voices, stems, uploads. **Premier ≈ $24/mo annual\n($30):** 10,000 credits + Studio — same legal rights as Pro; the extra buys volume + tooling. Subscription\ncredits don't roll over; top-ups persist but need an active sub. Known frictions (community-reported):\nPersona/Voice regressions after the May 2026 update; Studio's failed stem regenerations still deducting\ncredits; ISRC self-upload blocking on users' own distributed tracks.\n\n## Performance honesty\nOne analytics firm's campaign data (Chartlex, 2,400+ campaigns) reports fully-AI tracks underperform human\nreleases on Spotify save rate ~25–40% and completion ~15–25% — AI music is leverage for content and brand\nsound, not a shortcut to a streaming career. House metrics: human-ears fitness for the brief + the performance\nof the content it scores (native) — never fabricated.\n"
